  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the matrix $F=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find ${F}^{3}.$\n\n| ${F}^{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |",
  "worked_solution": "First, to find ${F}^{2},$ we multiply $F$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{2} & =F\\cdot F \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${F}^{3}={F}^{2}\\cdot F,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{3} & ={F}^{2}\\cdot F \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 36 \\\\ 18 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
